  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nPersistent upper high remains fixed over the Southern Rockies, \nand will sit there through at least the remainder of late July. \nThis will drive the weather pattern through the week ahead. GFS \n500-mb height anomalies suggest this high is 3-4 standard \ndeviations stronger than is typical for the middle of the North \nAmerican Monsoon. The pattern is not favorable for widespread \nrain, as any storms that do form will be mostly driven by recycled\nmoisture and convective temperature/terrain forcing producing \nmeager instability. The monsoonal plume will be broad and diffuse,\nbut generally favor the AZ/NM state line. Precipitable water \nvalues are around an inch through much of southwest New Mexico, \nwith drier air further to the east across far west Texas and \nsoutheast New Mexico.\n\nDowntrend in storm chances is expected over the next couple days,\nas the upper high drifts south slightly. This will put it directly\nover central New Mexico, acting as a suppressant with subsident\n500-mb flow causing a warming effect aloft and eating away at\npotential instability. Southwest New Mexico and the high terrain\nmay still see isolated (10-30% coverage) showers and thunderstorms\nWednesday/Thursday afternoons, but the majority of the CWA will\nremain dry.\n\nThe main headline for the desert lowlands will continue to be hot\ntemperatures. Suffocating upper ridge aloft will promote very hot\nafternoon conditions, followed by little relief overnight. Highs \nwill run 8-12 degrees above normal for late July, which means \nlowland highs in the 102 to 106 degree range through at least next\nFriday. A Heat Advisory is in effect for El Paso and the lower \nRio Grande Valley through Friday, and may need to be expanded back\ninto much of southern New Mexico on Friday.\n\nOverall weather pattern for the extended forecast will be warmer-\nthan-normal temperatures and isolated rain chances focused mainly\nacross southwest New Mexico and the southern NM high elevations. \nGFS/ECMWF both agree in a slight retrograde of the upper high as \nwe head into the first week of August, which may allow for \nbetter storm coverage and some interesting north flow aloft this\nupcoming Saturday/Sunday.",
